The reviewed code is TypeScript client-side subscription and risk/margin computation logic. Even though it is not an on-chain Solana program, it is security-relevant because it can drive automated trading/liquidation decisions and can be attacked via malformed/missing RPC data or edge-case account states.

From the provided CWE candidates, the main issues stem from:
- multiple unchecked divisors that can become zero (CWE-369), producing runtime exceptions or invalid calculations;
- missing handling for undefined/empty positions (CWE-755), leading to crashes when a position is absent;
- lossy conversion from BN (big integers) to JS number (CWE-704 / CWE-1339), which can silently corrupt comparisons and state updates;
- incomplete handling of account deletion / null accountInfo updates on websocket (CWE-431), leaving stale cached state and never notifying consumers;
- an unsubscribe method that can return undefined despite a Promise<void> signature (CWE-393), causing callers to treat an unsubscribe as successful while skipping expected cleanup.
